## a b s t r a c t
Fe based metal organic framework (MOF) materials are being extensively investigated as a precursor sample for engineering carbon supported iron containing nanoparticles composites. Rational design and engineering Fe-containing MOFs with optimized structures using economic and eco-friendly methods is a challenging task. In this work, 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid ( ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{{9H}}}_{6}{\mathrm{{O}}}_{6}$ trimesic acid, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}_{\mathsf{\ell}}^{\bullet}$ and metal Fe are employed to synthesize a MOF sample Fe-BTC in a mild hydrothermal condition. Moreover, with the addition of a small quantity of graphene oxide (GO) as dispersant, a redox coprecipitation reaction has taken place where small Fe-BTC domains well dispersed by reduced graphene oxide (RGO). The Fe-BTC/RGO intermediate sample is finally converted to the hierarchical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite, which delivers an ultrahigh specific capacity of $1262.61\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 150 cycles and a superior reversible capacity of $910.65~\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $1000\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 300 cycles in half cells. The full cell performance for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@C_{i}$ /RGO composite have been studied. It is also revealed that the improved structural stability, high pseudocapacitive contribution and enhanced lithium-ion and electron transportation conditions jointly guarantee the outstanding lithium-ion storage performances for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/\mathrm{RGO}$ composite over long-time cycling. The synthesized samples have good potential for wider application. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
As a conversion typed anode candidate for lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), magnetite $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\right)$ has a higher specific capacity of 926 $\mathrm{m}\mathsf{A h}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in theory than the $372\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for graphitic counterpart. However, challenges of low electrical conductivity and poor bulk cycling stability are urgently needed to overcome [1–4]. Conductive carbonaceous supporting matrices, such as graphene [5,6], carbon nanotubes [7,8], and porous carbons [9,10] are being widely used to immobilize $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ crystals with reduced size, thus improved electrochemical performances could be obtained for the rationally engineered composite materials. It is particularly meaningful to construct hierarchical carbonaceous frameworks with two or more carbon forms to achieve reinforced microstructure via simplified approaches.
Metal organic framework (MOF) materials are an emerging precursor for synthesizing porous carbon confining metal-containing nanocrystals due to their merits of high porosity and selftemplate effect in pyrolysis. The organic ligands play the vital role for design and fabrication of MOF materials [11–13]. Cost-effective and water-soluble 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{9}H_{6}O_{6}}$ , trimesic acid, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ is a class of rigid polycarboxylic acid-based ligand and capable to bond with metal ion centers in specific directions to form a unique extended MOF typed network structure with high porosity [14–16]. Among the corresponding MOF materials, ferric 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (Fe-BTC) has received wide attentions in many fields, such as hydrogen storage, gas adsorption, biomedical applications and catalysis [17–20]. Moreover, Fe-BTC is one of the most ideal pyrolysis precursor materials for further synthesizing porous carbon supported nanosized iron/iron oxide composite materials [21–23]. For example, Wezendonk and Gascon et. al have investigated the structural, electronic, and local environments of Fe evolutions for Fe-BTC MOF during pyrolysis and employed the pyrolyzed ${\mathrm{Fe@C}}$ composite as a highly active and stable catalyst in Fischer-Tropsch synthesis [24]. However, to fully exploit the FeBTC derived composite materials in electrochemical energy conversion and storage field, the excessive growth and spontaneous aggregation for the Fe-BTC MOF precursor have to be properly addressed.
Two-dimensional graphene oxide (GO) is a graphene-derived material with rich oxygen-containing functional groups on its surface and edge sites, which can be synthesized from graphite flakes [25,26]. GO is featured with high hydrophilicity and good processability, and GO could be reduced by various methods to form reduced graphene oxide (RGO) as an effective dispersant for smaller sized species [27–29]. On the other hand, it is critical to inhibit the over-restacking of the RGO layers from the $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions after the elimination of the hydrophilic radicals on GO sheets [30–32]. In this work, a simple and effective approach has been designed to simultaneously engineer $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles and pyrolyzed amorphous carbons well dispersed on RGO sheets as a hierarchical composite material $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}})$ . As shown in Scheme 1, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ molecules and metal Fe foils are employed as raw materials for synthesizing Fe-BTC MOF precursor in a mild aqueous redox condition. With the addition of a small quantity of few-layered GO, Fe-BTC domains with significantly controlled grain sizes are well dispersed by the RGO layers in the Fe-BTC/ RGO intermediate sample. After thermal treatment, both the excessive aggregation for small $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ domains and the overrestacking of large RGO sheets are effectively inhibited in the final hierarchical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ delivers an attractive specific capacity of $1262.61\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 150 cycles and a superior reversible capacity of 910.65 $\mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1000\ \mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 300 cycles in half cells. The lithium-ion storage mechanism and full cell performance of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample have been studied.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Reagents and materials
$\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ with a purity of $98\%$ was purchased from Accela Chembio without further purification. Iron foils $\cdot0.15\mathrm{mm}$ thick, $99.99\%$ 1 were supplied by Hunan Metal Materials, China. GO was synthesized from natural graphite flake (Sigma Aldrich, 325 mesh) according to our previous published method [33].
## 2.2. Sample synthesis
$\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ (1.0 g) and several polished iron foils were added into $150~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. The system was maintained magnetic stirring in an oil bath at $90~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After freezing drying the sediment, intermediate MOF sample Fe-BTC was prepared. The Fe-BTC sample was calcined at $500~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ in an Ar protected atmosphere to obtain the black colored $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ sample.
$\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ $_{(1.0\mathrm{~g})}$ was dissolved by $50~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. GO $\left(0.1\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ was added to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ deionized water to obtain a homogeneous suspension assisted by a $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ ultrasonication treatment. Then the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ solution and GO suspension was gradually mixed, and polished iron foils were added into the resulting mixture. The intermediate Fe-BTC/RGO and final $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ samples were synthesized following the same strategies as the Fe-BTC and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ samples, respectively.
## 2.3. Sample characterization and electrochemical measurement
## 2.3.1. Sample characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra was performed on an X-ray diffractometer (Rigaku Ultima IV) equipped with a $\mathsf{C u K}\mathsf{\ensuremath{\upalpha}}$ radiation $(\lambda~=~0.15418~\mathrm{{nm})}$ . The morphology and microstructure of the resulting samples were characterized by the field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, JEOL JSM-7800F) with energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) analysis and a transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL JEM-2100Plus). Renishaw inVia Plus MicroRaman spectroscopy system equipped with a $50\mathrm{mW}$ DPSS laser at $532~\mathrm{nm}$ was employed so as to record Raman spectra of the samples. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) data and derivative thermogravimetry (DTG) were collected by a thermal gravimetric analyzer (Mettler Toledo TGA-2) in air with a heating rate of 10 $\mathsf{^{c}m i n}^{-1}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI Quantera II) was measured to determine the chemical valence states of samples. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were characterized by the Autosorb-IQ-MP/XR surface area and pore analyzer (Quantachrome). The specific surface area was determined by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method and pore size distribution was obtained using the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) measurement based on the adsorption result.
## 2.3.2. Electrochemical measurement
For the preparation of the electrode, firstly, electrochemical active material $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C/R G O}$ or $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ ), carbon black, carbon fiber and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) were mixed in the weight ratio of 7:1:1:1. Appropriate amount of 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) was then added to form a homogeneous slurry with moderate viscosity, which was coated on current collector (copper/aluminum foil) and dried using an infrared lamp for about $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the initial drying, the loaded copper/aluminum foil was placed in a vacuum oven and dried at $110\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ overnight before cutting to obtain electrode discs with a diameter of $10~\mathrm{mm}$ . The electrode discs were weighed and marked separately, and the weight of the blank copper/aluminum disc was weighed at the same time to calculate the weight of electrochemical active material.
Coin-type CR2016 half cells were used to evaluate the electrochemical performance with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ nanocomposite as the working electrode and lithium foil as the reference electrode. Moreover, coin-type CR2025 full cells were used to test the cycling performances of the sample, where the commercial ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ sample was used as the cathode active material and aluminum foil was used as the current collector. For both half cells and full cells, the assembly of the cells were carried out under Ar-filled condition, and the operation required strict control of the oxygen and water content. The Celgard 2600 film was used as the separator between the anode and cathode. The electrolyte consists of $1.0\mathrm{M}$ lithium hexafluorophosphate $(\mathrm{LiPF}_{6})$ as the solute and ethylene carbonate (EC): dimethyl carbonate (DMC): ethylene methyl carbonate (EMC) with $1.0\%$ vinylene carbonate (VC) in a volumetric ratio as the solvent.
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were performed on a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation. Galvanostatic charge and discharge testing was carried out on a Neware battery test instrument and the specific capacity was calculated based on the mass of the synthesized composite samples. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) testing was also carried out using the CHI 660E electrochemical workstation.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure and morphology
The crystalline structures of Fe-BTC, Fe-BTC/RGO, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}_{I}$ RGO samples are first studied based on XRD characterization. From Fig. 1, the apparent diffraction peaks locating at 10.16, 10.90, 20.06 and 23.98 degree two theta are characteristic MOF structure in the Fe-BTC sample [15,34,35]. The XRD pattern also suggests that the Fe-BTC/RGO sample has similar diffraction peaks as the Fe-BTC sample with the addition of GO sheets in the reaction system. Moreover, the broad characteristic diffraction peak appears around 25.50 degree two theta is highly related to the RGO structure. The above XRD results verify that Fe-BTC MOF domains have been successfully engineered on the RGO sheets for the Fe-BTC/RGO sample via redox coprecipitation reactions under mild hydrothermal condition. After thermal treatment, apparent similar diffraction peaks could be seen in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ samples, and the XRD peaks at 30.10, 35.42, 43.05, 53.39, 56.94 and 62.52 degree two theta are assigned to the (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (4 0 0), (42 2), (5 1 1) and (4 4 0) crystal planes of magnetite face-centered cubic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ (JCPDS No. 19–0629), respectively. The enhanced peak intensity for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample indicates better crystallization for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles. Based on the Scherrer formula, the average sizes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ crystal from the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/C$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{R G O}$ samples are estimated to be about 14.68 and $12.86~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively.
FESEM images are used to investigate the morphological differences of the samples before and after thermal treatment. In Fig. 2a, irregular micro-sized particles can be seen with apparent aggregation for the Fe-BTC intermediate sample. In Fig. 2b, after thermal treatment, the overall morphology is largely maintained for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\C$ sample with slight size shrinkage, which is highly related to the inherent properties of MOF precursor. From the locally magnified FESEM image of the same sample in Fig. 2c, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles and pyrolyzed carbons can be clearly observed. However, the over exposure of aggregated $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles is probably unbeneficial for the enhancement of lithium-ion storage. By contrast, with the addition of small quantity of GO in the above reaction system, significantly optimized microstructures have been achieved in the Fe-BTC/RGO and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ samples. From Fig. 2d, large sized RGO sheets are clearly seen in the Fe-BTC/ RGO intermediate sample with small sized Fe-BTC MOF domains well dispersed. In Fig. 2e, for the corresponding pyrolyzed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ C/RGO sample, crinkled RGO framework can be observed without severe restacking. No apparent aggregates can be found in this sample, implying the effective dispersion effect for the RGO sheets. Moreover, from the higher magnification FESEM image in Fig. 2f, it can be seen $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C$ domains below $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ are uniformly dispersed with clear void space between these $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@C$ domains. The hierarchical carbon framework and well dispersed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite not only promotes the charge transfer, but also shortens the ionic transport pathway during lithium-ion storage. The EDS analysis result for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ $C/\mathrm{RGO}$ sample is shown in Fig. 2g. Carbon, oxygen and iron elements have been detected. Particularly, the densities of oxygen and iron elements are in mutual consistency, indicating most of oxygen element is in the form of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The elemental contents of carbon, oxygen and iron are identified with the atomic ratio of around $83.3\ \%$ , $12.6\ \%$ and $4.1\ \%$ , respectively. The extra quantity of oxygen element is speculated from the residual functional groups on carbon framework.
Based on the TEM image of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample in Fig. 3a, it is obvious that a large number of pyrolyzed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@C$ domains are well anchored on the RGO layers, and void space can be identified between the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\ensuremath{\mathrm O}_{4}}@C$ domains. This result corresponds well with the FESEM images in Fig. 2e and 2f. From Fig. 3b, corrugated few-layered RGO structures can be clearly observed with scattered nanoparticles below $10\mathrm{nm}$ on the surface. While for the magnified $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@C$ domains in Fig. 3c, the small sized nanoparticles about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ are well distributed in the amorphous carbons without apparent agglomeration. From the HRTEM image in Fig. 3d, clear lattice stripes are observed on the immobilized individual nanoparticle. The crystal plane spacing is measured to be $0.254~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , matching with the (311) directions of crystalline $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ . This result confirms the successful engineering of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ $C/\mathrm{RGO}$ composite with unique hierarchical structure.
Raman spectroscopy testing results of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ RGO samples are presented in Fig. 4a. Two distinct peaks can be seen at about $1340\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (D-band) and $1596\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (G-band), corresponding to the defective/disordered sp3 correlation carbon and ordered sp2 correlation graphitic carbon, respectively [36,37]. The ratio of the peak intensity between D-band and G-band $\mathrm{(I_{D}/}$ ${\mathrm{I}}_{\mathrm{G}}{\mathrm{,}}$ offers a valuable measure for evaluating the degree of crystallinity of various carbon materials. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratios are measured to be 0.26 and 1.05 for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\C$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ RGO composites, respectively. The significantly higher $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ is ascribed to the result that GO has been reduced and finally an RGO coating layer has been engineered [38,39].
Fig. 4b exhibits the TGA and corresponding DTG curves of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\C$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ samples. For $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\C$ sample, it can be observed that an obvious weight fading between 250 degreesC and 400 degreesC from the TGA curve, and the highest weight loss rate is measured at $392.5~\mathrm{^\circC}$ from the DTG result. This mass loss event is speculated to the decomposition of the pyrolyzed amorphous carbon species. By contrast, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample undergoes a different weight loss event. The mass decline of the $\mathrm{Fe3O4@C/RGO}$ sample can be divided into two stages, which is probably originated from the decomposition of the pyrolyzed amorphous carbon domains and RGO sheets. The inflection points of the two stages are measured to be $392.5^{\circ}\mathrm{~\bar{C}~}$ and $480.8^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ respectively. Therefore, the corresponding $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ content in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ are $74.71\%$ and $69.30\%$ , respectively, and the carbon framework in the two samples are $25.29\ \%$ and $23.58\ \%$ , respectively. In particular, the RGO component in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ RGO sample is estimated to be $7.12\%$ .
Fig. 4c-4f exhibit the XPS analysis of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample. According to the wide-scan XPS survey spectra in Fig. 4c, the atomic percentage of carbon, oxygen and iron elements can be measured to 62.76, 24.14 and 13.10, respectively. In Fig. 4d, the C 1 s spectrum at higher resolution for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{R G O}$ sample has two obvious fitted peaks located at the binding energy of at 284.60 and $285.30\mathrm{~eV}$ , standing for the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ and $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ bonds, respectively. The fitted smaller peak at about $286.23\:\mathrm{eV}$ is ascribed to the $c{-}0$ bond from RGO while a neglectable peak at $289.14\mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to $0{\cdot}C{=}0$ functional groups [33]. The fitting results of O 1 s is shown in Fig. 4e. The Fe-O peak locates at $530.34\mathrm{eV}$ indicates the existence of lattice oxygen atoms in $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ surface structure. The peak at $531.26\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be assign to the FeAOAC bond, indicating the improved stability for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanocrystals and the carbonaceous matrix [40]. The $\scriptstyle\mathtt{C=}0/0\mathrm{-}\mathtt{C=}0$ peak at $532.60\ \mathrm{eV}$ and the C-OH peak at 533.57 eV with remarkably less intensities are attributed from the residual functional groups from the carbon skeleton [41]. Fig. 4f presents the Fe 2p spectrum for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ . The two peaks at 724.70 and $711.20\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the $2{\tt p}1/2$ and $2{\tt p}3/2$ orbitals of Fe, respectively. Moreover, due to the presence of dual oxidation states such as $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , the Fe 2p1/2 and Fe $2{\tt p}3/2$ can be divided into two parts, so the fitted peaks at 723.98 and $710.61\ \mathrm{eV}$ are related to Fe 2p1/2 and Fe $2{\tt p}3/2$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , while the fitting peaks at 725.86 and $712.48\mathrm{eV}$ belong to Fe $2{\mathsf{p}}1/2$ and Fe 2p3/2 in $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . The above result indicates that the iron oxide phase is $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ [42].
Fig. $4{\mathrm{g}}$ show the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms to characterize the specific surface area of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ . Based on the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption data, the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample is calculated to be $83.50~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In Fig. 3h, the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) pore distribution curve of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ indicates the rich mesopores in this sample. This mesoporous structure can not only function as a buffering space for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ crystals, but promote the ionic transport with a small resistance [43].
From the above characterization results, it can be seen the reaction between the Fe foils, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ molecules and GO sheets in mild hydrothermal condition contributes to the formation of the FeBTC/RGO intermediate sample with improved microstructure, which leads to the formation of the hierarchical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample. The possible reaction mechanism between Fe foils, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ and GO aqueous suspension is proposed in Scheme 2. In the mild aqueous condition, the redox reaction between the Fe foils and GO sheets leads to the formation of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions dispersing in water phase and hydrophobic solid RGO sheets. The RGO species has good affinity with the dissolved ${\mathsf{B T C}}^{3-}$ groups due to the $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, and the ${\mathsf{B T C}}^{3-}$ groups could be well dispersed on the RGO sheets. The following chelation reaction between the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions and the ${\mathsf{B T C}}^{3-}$ groups generate the Fe-BTC MOFs, which in-situ decorated on the RGO sheets. The RGO sheets significantly inhibit the severe aggregation for the Fe-BTC domains, in return, the small FeBTC domains effectively prevent the spontaneous restacking for the RGO sheets. Therefore, optimized structures for the final $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - $0_{4}@C/\mathsf{R G O}$ sample can be achieved after thermal treatment of the Fe-BTC/RGO intermediate sample, which will contribute to enhanced electrochemical performance in lithium-ion storage.
## 3.2. Lithium-ion storage performances
Fig. 5a exhibits the CV curves for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite. A significantly different reduction peak at approximately $\phantom{-}0.74\:\mathrm{V}$ can be noticed in the initial cycle, which is probably ascribed to the reduction of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ with the formation of metallic Fe phase and accompanied by the production of amorphous $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ matrix as well as the generation of a partially irreversible solid electrolyte interface (SEI) [44,45]. Meanwhile, for the anodic scan, the oxidation of metallic Fe is demonstrated by two anodic peaks at 1.63 and $1.87\mathrm{V}$ [46]. The cathodic part of the CV curve for the subsequent cycles, the reduction peak shifts slightly to a higher voltage of about $_{0.81\mathrm{~V~}}$ , which can be mainly ascribed to the improved kinetics, originates from the nanometer-sized effect inherent in transition metal oxide electrodes during cycling [47]. It is noteworthy that the CV curves almost overlap each other in the subsequent cycles, implying the well structural stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ RGO samples [48].
Fig. 5b exhibits the 1st, 2nd, 3rd and 100th discharge and charge curves of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite electrode at a low current of $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite exhibits a high initial discharge specific capacity of $1669.63\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and a charge specific capacity of $1098.23\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in the first cycle, so that the initial coulombic efficiency can be calculated to be $65.78\%$ . In addition, an obvious voltage plateau at about $0.75\mathrm{V}$ can be seen from the first discharge curve, while from the 2nd and 3rd discharge curves, the voltage plateau moves to a slightly higher position. For the charge curves for the first 3 cycles, the corresponding voltage plateau could be found between 1.5 and $2.0~\mathrm{V}.$ . These results match quite well with the CV curves in Fig. 5a. By contrast, there are obvious offsets for the charge/discharge curves of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@$ -
$C/\mathrm{RGO}$ composite electrode after 100 cycles, implying a gradual changed lithium-ion storage mechanism. It has been reported the generation and transformation of Fe atoms from the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ crystals would trigger the formation of a conductive polymeric film, which could contribute to additional lithium-ion storage capacity through pseudocapacitive behavior with cycling [49,50].
Fig. 6a compares the cycling performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}$ $0_{4}@C/\mathsf{R G O}$ composite electrodes at a low current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The initial specific discharge capacities for the two samples are 1509.46 and $1669.63\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. However, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite delivers a significantly higher initial specific charge capacity of $1098.23~\mathrm{\mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ than the 697.16 $\mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ composite. This result also means the superior structure stability for the hierarchical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite. The reversible capacities of both electrodes exhibit a moderate decrease and then turn to a gradual rising trend in subsequent cycles, possibly ascribe to the stable SEI film, complex electrochemical activation process and the enhanced lithium-ion diffusion kinetics during cycling [51]. At the 150th cycle, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}.$ $0_{4}@C_{I}$ RGO electrode delivers a high reversible capacity of $1262.61\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , while the reversible capacity of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\C$ electrode is $602.80\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Fig. 6b compares the long-time lithiumion storage performances of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\C$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{R G O}$ composite electrodes at a high current density of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . For the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}$ $0_{4}@C/\mathsf{R G O}$ electrode, it can be seen that the initial charge and discharge capacities are 954.02 and $1484.98\:\:\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ with a coulombic efficiency of $64.24\%$ . At the 300th cycle, a high specific capacity about $910.65\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}}^{-1}$ can be delivered, which is much higher than the $106.94\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/C$ electrode. A comparison of the rate capability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ is shown in Fig. 6c. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ electrode provides a higher specific capacity than $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/C$ electrode at each stage. For the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ RGO electrode, the average reversible capacities under current densities of 100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000 and $10000\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are about 1136.30, 1088.46, 986.92, 898.70, 805.15, 586.24, $317.39\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}}^{-1}$ , respectively. When current density is restored to the initial current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , this electrode still has a high reversible capacity of about $1221.01\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .
Table 1 compared the electrochemical test results of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ RGO samples with the recently published literature. Considering the specific capacity, cycle times and current rates, it is obvious that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}_{I}$ RGO composite offers a competitive lithium-ion storage behavior.
Cyclic voltammetry can not only analyze the electrochemical reaction of electrode, but also further study the pseudocapacitive effect of lithium ions. As shown in Fig. 7a, the seven CV curves have similar shapes as the scan rate gradually increases, indicating that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ has attractive electrochemical reversibility performance. The charge storage kinetics of the electrodes can be described according to the equation between the peak current (i) and the scan rate $(\nu)$ , which obeys the following power-law formula: $i=\mathsf{a}\nu^{\mathsf{b}}$ [62], where $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{b}}$ is a constant that can be obtained by plotting ln (i) against ln $(\nu)$ . A b value of 0.5 indicates that diffusion is the main contribution to lithium storage, while a b value of 1.0 suggests that capacitive behavior dominates the entire reaction [63]. Fig. 7b shows ln (i)-ln $(\nu)$ for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{R G O}$ electrode. The b values for the cathodic and anodic peaks are 0.75 and 0.85, indicating that the lithium-ion storage kinetics is governed by both pseudocapacitive and diffusion processes at slow scan rates of 0.1 to $1.0\:\mathrm{mV}\cdot\varsigma^{-1}$ [64]. The capacitive contribution can be further elaborated on the basis of the following equation: $i=\mathbf{k}_{1}\nu+\mathbf{k}_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ , where $\mathbf{k}_{1}\nu$ and $\mathrm{k}_{2}\upsilon^{1/2}$ denote the specific contributions from capacitivecontrolled and diffusion-controlled, respectively [65]. The values of $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ can be obtained based on the slope and intercept of the line fitting of $i/\nu^{1/2}$ and $\nu^{1/2}$ under a specific potential [66]. Based on the quantitative analysis results, Fig. 7c shows the ratio of capacitive contribution for different scanning rates. On the one hand, the high capacitive contribution comes from the pseudocapacitive behavior generated by lithium ions reacting with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles and adsorbing on the oxygen-containing functional groups of the carbon matrix [67]. On the other hand, the presence of abundant pores accelerates the electrolyte penetration and expands the contact area between the active material and the electrolyte, thus providing the surface pseudocapacitive contribution [68]. Obviously, the significantly higher contribution of the pseudocapacitive behavior to the diffusion-controlled behavior at higher scan rates can be seen. As the sweep rate increases, the capacitive contribution gradually increases from $71.5\ \%$ to $88.4\ \%$ Therefore, the fast surface electrochemical reaction with little structural degradation of the active material result in excellent multiplicity performance and long cycle stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ electrodes [69].
To further investigate the diffusion coefficient of lithium ions in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite electrode, constant current intermittent titration technique (GITT) measurements have been performed. The GITT curve of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{R G O}$ electrode during the first cycle is shown in Fig. 7d. The cell is first discharged at a constant current density of $200\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and then rest intervals for $20\mathrm{min}$ to bring the cell voltage to near equilibrium (vs $\mathrm{Li^{+}}/\protect$ Li). This process has been repeated for the entire operating potential range until reaching the final cut off voltage of $0.01\mathrm{~V~}$ . For the charging process, the cell is run at the same current density and relaxation time and the setup is repeated until the final potential limit is reached. Based on the Fick’s second law, the apparent lithium ion diffusion coefficient $(D_{L i}^{+})$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}_{I}$ RGO can be calculated as follows: $\begin{array}{r}{D_{L i^{+}}=\frac{4}{\pi\tau}\Big(\frac{m_{B}V_{m}}{M_{B}S}\Big)^{2}\Big(\frac{\Delta E_{S}}{\Delta E_{\tau}}\Big)^{2}}\end{array}$ , where $\tau$ is the pulse time, mB, $V_{m}$ and $M_{B}$ are the mass, molar volume and molecular weight of the electrode material, respectively. S is the area of electrode plate. $\varDelta E_{S}$ and $\varDelta E_{\tau}$ represent the change in the equilibrium potential and the change in potential during the current pulse, respectively [70–
72]. Fig. 7e exhibits an amplified GITT curve, which described schematically described the meanings of $\tau$ $\varDelta E_{S}$ and $\varDelta E_{\tau}$ . The calculated results of $D_{L i}^{+}$ are shown in Fig. 7f, the average value of $D_{L i}^{+}$ at lithiation state (discharge process) is $9.92\times10^{-10}\mathrm{{cm}}^{2}{\cdot}5^{-1}$ , and the average value of $D_{L i}^{+}$ at delithiation state (charge process) is $1.07\times10^{-9}10\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ . The excellent lithium-ion diffusion benefits from a hierarchical carbon support structure, which offers a large electrode/electrolyte contact area and an effective lithium-ion diffusion pathway.
Fig. 8a-8d are the FESEM images of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ electrode before and after 100 cycles. It can be clearly seen that the overall morphology and microstructures of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ electrode are largely maintained except that an SEI film is formed on the surface. The Nyquist plots with inset equivalent circuit model are shown in Fig. 8e. The shapes in the Nyquist diagram before and after the 100 cycles are semicircles connected by a straight line. The semicircle is related to the high-frequency and mid-frequency regions. The intercept of the high-frequency region at $Z^{\prime}$ axis refers to the inherent resistance of the cell (Rs). The high-frequency region corresponds to charge transfer resistor (Rct), respectively. The lowfrequency region is associated with the Warburg impedance (Zw) [73–76]. In addition, the low-frequency region represents the ion diffusion resistance in the electrolyte. Obviously, the diameter of the semi-circle in the high frequency region of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite electrode after 100 cycles is significantly reduced, indicating that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite electrode has a smaller charge transfer resistance, which can enhance the electronic conductivity.
With a view to the commercial potential of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample, full cells have been assembled by integrating the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ $C/\mathrm{RGO}$ anode with a commercially available ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ cathode, and the schematic illustration is shown in Fig. 9a. As shown in Fig. 9b and 9c, the $\mathsf{L i F e P O_{4}}$ electrode has an apparent discharge voltage plateau at about $3.3\mathrm{~V~}$ and delivers a specific capacity of approximately $120\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles in half cells. Fig. 9d exhibits the galvanostatic charge/discharge voltage curves of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@C/$ $\mathsf{R G O}//\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}$ full cells between 0.2 and $3.5\mathrm{~V~}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . For the 1st cycle, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite presents an excellent charge specific capacity of $1324.65\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and a discharge specific capacity of $1142.33\mathrm{\mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , and the initial coulombic efficiency could be calculated to be $86.24\ \%$ . In the following cycles, the outlines of the curves are quite similar except for a slight capacity fading. As shown in Fig. 9e, the discharge capacity remains $614.68~\mathrm{\mAh{cdotg}}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample, which is about $53.80\ \%$ of the first discharge capacity. There are many factors that may cause the capacity decay of the full cell, such as the current density mismatch between the two electrodes and the irrational electrode mass ratio [77].
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a hierarchical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ composite has been successfully engineered by redox co-precipitation reaction and a subsequent thermal treatment. Cost-effectiveness $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ molecules, metal Fe foils and a small quantity of GO sheets are used as starting materials, which contributes to the formation of a FeBTC/RGO intermediate sample with favorable structure. The lithium-ion storage performances of the Fe-BTC MOF derived $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - $0_{4}@C/\mathsf{R G O}$ sample are investigated for the first time. As a result, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample delivers an ultrahigh specific capacity of $1262.61\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 150 cycles and a superior reversible capacity of $910.65\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}}^{-1}$ at $1000\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 300 cycles in half cells, which are quite competitive over the recently reported carbonaceous matrix supported $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ materials in terms of the specific capacity, cycle times and current rates [52–61]. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{R G O}}$ sample could still have a high discharge capacity of $614.68~\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{RGO}//\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}$ full cells between 0.2 and $3.6~\mathrm{V}$ at $200\ \mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The unique hierarchical microstructure, the significantly improved lithium-ion and electron diffusion condition contribute to the superior electrochemical behaviors. The synthesized materials have good potential for wider application.
